cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  agent-channel-observability: measuring reasoning-channel emission
  and the recall ceiling it imposes on deliberation-gated monitors
message: >-
  If you use this software or its measurements, please cite it. Please also
  carry the caveats: every rate is per arm (model, task class, request
  configuration and provider route) and is not a sample of vendors; the recall
  drop is measured on a model that is not a clean behavioural A/B (the /no_think
  arm also raised refusals); the long-horizon and peer-objection results are
  suggestive and a bounded null respectively, not powered; and the peer-objection
  endpoints (C1-C3) were pre-specified but not independently pre-registered.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Johanna
    family-names: Angulo
repository-code: "https://github.com/Jangulo7/agent-channel-observability"
abstract: >-
  Measures how often a model's reasoning is actually readable by an external
  evaluator, and derives the recall ceiling that readability imposes on any
  monitor gated on deliberation. The headline result is that whether reasoning
  is readable is set by the serving configuration rather than by the model:
  across a nine-arm reasoning-model sweep the same model moves from 0 to 98%
  readable on one request parameter, and in a controlled agentic experiment
  post-tool-call reasoning goes from 0 of 661 turns to 667 of 667 readable on a
  single provider beta header, with model and task held fixed. On harmful-graded
  trajectories a monitor reading only the reasoning channel loses recall (0.93 to
  0.00) when that channel is dark, while a monitor reading only the actions is
  largely unaffected: the coverage-to-recall collapse, measured rather than
  assumed. Six corpora (23,541 assistant turns) are measured separately and never
  pooled; the paper reports three of them and keeps the rest as appendix material.
  Provider token accounting separates reasoning that was not produced from
  reasoning that was produced but not returned. Two rules are architectural: no
  detector reports a rate without a recorded validated recall, and an
  uninspectable sample is a failure rather than a dropped denominator. No corpus
  text is committed; only counts, rates, figures and the records, and every
  reported number regenerates from a committed record.
keywords:
  - AI safety
  - evaluation
  - chain-of-thought monitorability
  - observability
  - monitor recall
  - Inspect
license: MIT
version: 1.0.1
date-released: "2026-09-14"

# After the GitHub release is archived to Zenodo, add the minted DOI here so
# `cff-version` tooling and Zenodo agree (the concept DOI resolves to all versions):
# identifiers:
#   - type: doi
#     value: "10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX"
#     description: "Concept DOI for all versions"

references:
  - type: software
    title: "safety-eval-pipeline"
    authors:
      - given-names: Johanna
        family-names: Angulo
    repository-code: "https://github.com/Jangulo7/safety-eval-pipeline"
    version: "v1.1.0"
    doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22182741"
    notes: >-
      Source of the vendored statistics helpers in src/channels/_vendored_stats.py
      and of this project's gate contract: an aggregate bound plus a per-stratum
      bound, the worst stratum always reported, a marginal annotation when the
      confidence interval crosses the threshold, and un-evaluable counting as
      failure rather than being dropped.

  - type: data
    title: "Mythos 5 incident transcript"
    authors:
      - name: Anthropic
    repository-code: "https://github.com/anthropics/mythos-5-incident-transcript"
    url: "https://www.anthropic.com/claude-fable-and-mythos-5-1"
    notes: >-
      One of six measured corpora in results/. Licence not
      stated; released by Anthropic for study. Carries a canary GUID and a
      do-not-train notice. n = 1 trajectory: one incident, one model, not a
      sample. See docs/DATA_PROVENANCE.md.
